#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157065 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"}] :question/id #uuid "813b7b5f-0da8-5a92-b4ff-f7208c5f059f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7654" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{x+3} + 1 \\geq  0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+3}+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+3} & \\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{x+3})}^{3} & \\ge {(-1)}^{3} \\\\ x+3 & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157066 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/id #uuid "bc44bc68-a615-5f25-b686-8a775fcac64c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7655" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3\\sqrt{x-2} +7 \\geq 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{x-2}+7 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx-2 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\geq 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157067 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/id #uuid "bca45b70-4e79-5baf-a4be-186366cebdd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7656" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $7-\\sqrt{x+3} < 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{x+3} & <5 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x+3} & <-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+3})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ x+3 & >4 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x > 1,$ which can be expressed as $\\left( 1,\\infty \\right).$ The domain of the square root is $x \\geq -3,$ which can be expressed as $\\left[ -3,\\infty \\right).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\left( 1,\\infty \\right) \\cap \\left[ -3,\\infty \\right)  =\\left( 1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in \\left( 1,\\infty\\right),$ which can also be written as $x > 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8c0064c-2169-55d7-a947-48d6d03ba17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282114} {:answer/id #uuid "8371a41b-3f05-5473-9afe-0f0222751d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282115} {:answer/id #uuid "46364e8a-fa81-5f7a-b7bc-9e8f5e7cb101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282116} {:answer/id #uuid "ea604ada-e0aa-5f07-9195-70669dac40a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282117} {:answer/id #uuid "e8477def-5adf-5765-91e0-ed8bd9429fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8c0064c-2169-55d7-a947-48d6d03ba17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282114} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e6918a3-0472-5925-80f6-f0d5b5cb777b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "887bbbbc-3fb4-56cf-816e-509931191e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115031" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}-1\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}-1 & \\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2x+1} & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{2x+1})}^{3} & \\le {3}^{3} \\\\ 2x+1 & \\le 27 \\\\ 2x & \\le 26 \\\\ x & \\le 13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eebf82f-6cbc-5263-808d-6b8f42e91a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282121} {:answer/id #uuid "e1acc0ce-e5b9-57ab-a210-aadafbd50d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282122} {:answer/id #uuid "890eb7bc-ae49-539d-85ce-ef8bcc899b4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282123} {:answer/id #uuid "684014aa-13d5-52e3-8227-803e19b15be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282124} {:answer/id #uuid "9c863c69-4e95-539e-8505-3c9f4b9676cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890eb7bc-ae49-539d-85ce-ef8bcc899b4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282123} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dfbe473-a6a5-53e1-a7be-c36c267f8de7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3674da94-bade-5994-825c-d5599c95c366" :question/math-academy-id "q-115044" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{7x-8}+5<1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7x-8}+5 & <1 \\\\ \\sqrt{7x-8} & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is less than a negative number $(-4),$ so no number that we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to the given inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f77af5f4-17af-5bd0-b2f6-b4c21847c184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f77af5f4-17af-5bd0-b2f6-b4c21847c184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282128} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f91c7e-8e27-5398-b946-e7c9b07e3372" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282127} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd3f57e-7d66-5461-8dbd-6397eb3639c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd3f57e-7d66-5461-8dbd-6397eb3639c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282130} :answer-field/id #uuid "2551328a-5b05-501e-96e8-16b3a07747e7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbe353ba-46fb-59a0-b576-0494179bee8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-276739" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $6-2\\sqrt{x-7}\\le 14.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-2\\sqrt{x-7} & \\le 14 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{x-7} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-7} & \\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-4),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx-7\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge 7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "973bfd43-7c9c-533f-975b-bbdbc76f7453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "973bfd43-7c9c-533f-975b-bbdbc76f7453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282133} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7f638f-e72a-5441-98cf-744e016c169e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282132} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec9b7982-e03b-58fa-9352-7b2331b68316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec9b7982-e03b-58fa-9352-7b2331b68316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282135} :answer-field/id #uuid "eade201c-1854-50dc-af12-22c5ed764b10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282134} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acec4555-fc54-5f17-832d-5bdc46b80ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acec4555-fc54-5f17-832d-5bdc46b80ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282137} :answer-field/id #uuid "c041bc04-e3f7-5c3f-b4a0-950a36e0cce4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "223ae82c-5013-5c43-be8d-095a6256031a" :question/math-academy-id "q-276744" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x}+6\\ge 2.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x}+6 & \\ge 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{4+5x} & \\ge -4 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+5x} & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(12).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4+5x})}^{2} & \\le {12}^{2} \\\\ 4+5x & \\le 144 \\\\ 5x & \\le 140 \\\\ x & \\le 28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+5x & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5x & \\ge -4 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 28,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,28].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{4}{5},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{4}{5},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,28]\\cap [-\\frac{4}{5},\\infty )=[-\\frac{4}{5},28]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{4}{5},28],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le 28.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1b74fb4-6ddc-5adf-8dbf-6ade7efa002b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b74fb4-6ddc-5adf-8dbf-6ade7efa002b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282140} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fffb395-401d-5c98-8952-68c18729e301" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282139} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45891d06-6b13-5d49-b7a7-f15983f7c603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45891d06-6b13-5d49-b7a7-f15983f7c603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282142} :answer-field/id #uuid "152a7b47-f4c2-5503-ab10-3160b1878750" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c07e2b0-f362-5e98-884d-345cda7607c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-277798" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2+\\sqrt[3]{7x+6}<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt[3]{7x+6} & <0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{7x+6} & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{7x+6})}^{3} & <(-2)^{3} \\\\ 7x+6 & <-8 \\\\ 7x & <-14 \\\\ x & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2cab593-b132-5749-a853-a01b4935697e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2cab593-b132-5749-a853-a01b4935697e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282145} :answer-field/id #uuid "fddd6989-448c-55a4-8250-f12cd3e28207" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983353ab-ebb6-55a5-aa1b-5afeaf616e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "983353ab-ebb6-55a5-aa1b-5afeaf616e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282147} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd9f98e-6e1d-5ad0-a82c-501015c73684" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1089c38f-a5cb-57d2-8baf-f6817712191d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277827" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $6-\\sqrt[3]{10+3x}<2.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\sqrt[3]{10+3x} & <2 \\\\ -\\sqrt[3]{10+3x} & <-4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{10+3x} & >4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{10+3x})}^{3} & >{4}^{3} \\\\ 10+3x & >64 \\\\ 3x & >54 \\\\ x & >18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0140b7c1-d776-53a9-95df-a442a623e362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282150} {:answer/id #uuid "2c620424-f556-52d3-b13f-bfe1b8325a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c620424-f556-52d3-b13f-bfe1b8325a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282151} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b24f09-dcd3-56d5-8b55-811827b976f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d697521d-28e9-5f40-9c0e-c9b61cb1cf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d697521d-28e9-5f40-9c0e-c9b61cb1cf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282153} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0dc669-054a-590d-9a13-78d5003e121f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d2b2038-da18-501d-bc90-955a0385a2f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-277833" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{6x-2}+8<10.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{6x-2}+8 & <10 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{6x-2} & <2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{6x-2} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{6x-2})}^{3} & <{4}^{3} \\\\ 6x-2 & <64 \\\\ 6x & <66 \\\\ x & <11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d729061-0aa2-52da-bf63-e484c81c8ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d729061-0aa2-52da-bf63-e484c81c8ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282156} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24c688d-2e02-52d9-96d1-c2c73d527a32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f704249-c3ed-5309-b980-c0cba9b4fa1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f704249-c3ed-5309-b980-c0cba9b4fa1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282158} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e70fb06-f6ee-572b-8358-0e96dae9f7f4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4bc119c-5594-5ada-be1a-d0b168425b8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-277834" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2-\\sqrt{6x-2}<10.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{6x-2} & <10 \\\\ -\\sqrt{6x-2} & <8 \\\\ \\sqrt{6x-2} & >-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-8),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n6x-2\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge \\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b32859f-edbf-5ad4-9500-542aff8a6153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282161} {:answer/id #uuid "f4114239-63e8-5706-84a7-8ee52c15cef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4114239-63e8-5706-84a7-8ee52c15cef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282162} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f156c37-8c7f-5da6-9153-295bb14ccf82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a917009c-79aa-5201-9fe5-4316d50e30db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a917009c-79aa-5201-9fe5-4316d50e30db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282164} :answer-field/id #uuid "e36dacf6-8087-5aa1-9f9e-496998d8eab0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a57a369-5817-5b7b-a405-748d786c4bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-277839" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{5-10x}+2>-7.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-10x}+2 & >-7 \\\\ \\sqrt{5-10x} & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-3),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-10x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -10x & \\ge -5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dda35516-e167-533c-8204-e42987c601c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282167} {:answer/id #uuid "a91016d4-e589-5a33-a570-00f832a111a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dda35516-e167-533c-8204-e42987c601c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282167} :answer-field/id #uuid "083b1abf-1512-5edb-b35c-c767b61150ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77431ccc-7adc-5d14-b13c-94c23d6507ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77431ccc-7adc-5d14-b13c-94c23d6507ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282170} :answer-field/id #uuid "beccbea7-027e-58b6-9612-6a7372aa6100" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60bbb0fa-98c4-5229-9ebc-272c622e1ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-278213" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2\\sqrt{3x+6}+11\\ge 5.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3x+6}+11 & \\ge 5 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3x+6} & \\ge -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+6} & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-3),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n3x+6\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "960790ca-fb43-5f26-bbdb-de33c364c053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "960790ca-fb43-5f26-bbdb-de33c364c053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282173} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccc6614d-401a-557c-8aef-2d0f40dbe061" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208fc795-9329-5451-b120-3fedec8b1f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "208fc795-9329-5451-b120-3fedec8b1f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282175} :answer-field/id #uuid "b95a4291-5d03-5add-8e28-34fcd52966ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d6dd5cb-4c74-554b-85e5-bd643ffeabd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-278280" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3+2\\sqrt{4x+1}>7.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2\\sqrt{4x+1} & >7 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{4x+1} & >4 \\\\ \\sqrt{4x+1} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4x+1})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ 4x+1 & >4 \\\\ 4x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\frac{3}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{1}{4},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )\\cap [-\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )=(\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{3}{4}<x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a6ae1d-653f-5cc2-a213-62be95607364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3} \\le x \\le \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156245} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f791b9-0b41-5fd3-bbc8-19ac994d83fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x \\le - \\frac{1}{3}$ or $x \\le \\frac{8}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156246} {:answer/id #uuid "2d070ea2-0273-5a28-bde8-5efe7044cfcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156247} {:answer/id #uuid "324eb938-4568-596c-bab5-f48f39a53f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge - \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156248} {:answer/id #uuid "35053ab5-6428-54c9-86eb-7782a78c26aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\le \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83a6ae1d-653f-5cc2-a213-62be95607364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3} \\le x \\le \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156245} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee517260-8643-55f5-b902-214156fc4d73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fefb96f6-18f6-5055-9c5e-8d69e5bbce1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87508" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1\\ge -5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{3x+1}+1 & \\ge -5 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3x+1} & \\ge -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+1} & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x+1})}^{2} & \\le {3}^{2} \\\\ 3x+1 & \\le 9 \\\\ 3x & \\le 8 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{8}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{8}{3}]\\cap [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )=[-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{1}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{8}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb4d9c9b-6b48-5ee9-ade3-701c9d1d05f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282178} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e7b1b5-851d-53e2-ba66-0ff1162752d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282179} {:answer/id #uuid "e946bcc1-97b9-5acb-84c0-1ddb101a796d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282180} {:answer/id #uuid "ced986ca-c9bf-5c3d-a168-96d7063e1139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282181} {:answer/id #uuid "f63b9fc8-40bf-59b8-8d31-8f32382a8847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e946bcc1-97b9-5acb-84c0-1ddb101a796d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282180} :answer-field/id #uuid "76bdca18-3c1d-50f0-8dfb-751020f97ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fac6f6b-777c-51eb-9932-13bc1cf1aee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-87626" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{4-3x}-2\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{4-3x}-2 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{4-3x} & \\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{4-3x})}^{3} & \\le {2}^{3} \\\\ 4-3x & \\le 8 \\\\ -3x & \\le 4 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we had to flip the sign of the inequality because we divided by a negative number.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf33c05-ef8f-5fe0-a50e-8235006ee86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282185} {:answer/id #uuid "dac86667-8b83-5b8c-a4fd-94a9f859864f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -\\frac{3}{4}$ or $x\\ge \\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282186} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7c803d-514f-5baf-b721-a063b47ea501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282187} {:answer/id #uuid "c0917dba-fd7e-5a23-bf95-50704b9789c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282188} {:answer/id #uuid "e080ab0a-819c-55ce-b441-7afe66114d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e080ab0a-819c-55ce-b441-7afe66114d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282189} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fbf1a9f-2593-52a8-bff8-0a838deaec41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35a96384-9344-589c-be4d-2b558d862974" :question/math-academy-id "q-87781" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2\\sqrt{4x+3}-13\\le -5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{4x+3}-13 & \\le -5 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{4x+3} & \\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{4x+3} & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(4).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{4x+3})}^{2} & \\le {4}^{2} \\\\ 4x+3 & \\le 16 \\\\ 4x & \\le 13 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -3 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{13}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{13}{4}].$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{3}{4},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{13}{4}]\\cap [-\\frac{3}{4},\\infty )=[-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{13}{4}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{13}{4}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{3}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{13}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b1a44e-4259-5e0e-ba76-08da2e647f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282192} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a0c96b-3ef4-53ef-99ba-ba0bd46c686f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282193} {:answer/id #uuid "0da205c2-25cd-5f2d-b0ce-956c63fbce67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -\\frac{2}{3}$   or  $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282194} {:answer/id #uuid "e3770220-b8a7-5e25-936f-87361d1cc209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282195} {:answer/id #uuid "41b5bab1-ceeb-51f8-9df0-9ff362ff1d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4a0c96b-3ef4-53ef-99ba-ba0bd46c686f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282193} :answer-field/id #uuid "2163ed60-ddb2-586b-ad9c-f76231dce3df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bff9f6f-9399-5272-973c-7c5134d6a566" :question/math-academy-id "q-87782" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $12-5\\sqrt{3x+2}\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-5\\sqrt{3x+2} & \\le 2 \\\\ -5\\sqrt{3x+2} & \\le -10 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x+2} & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x+2})}^{2} & \\ge {2}^{2} \\\\ 3x+2 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -2 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -\\frac{2}{3},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )\\cap [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )=[\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{2}{3}.$"}]}
